WEBVTT

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.836
Welcome to the last video.

00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:08.789
It covers communications and the tools you'll use.

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:09.735
First, HF radio.

00:00:09.735 --> 00:00:14.697
VHF is clear, but it works only in line of sight, about two hundred and fifty miles.

00:00:14.697 --> 00:00:16.964
Over the ocean, aircraft use HF radio,

00:00:16.964 --> 00:00:20.663
which bounces off the ionosphere and carries over the horizon.

00:00:20.663 --> 00:00:21.844
The price is static.

00:00:21.844 --> 00:00:23.262
HF isn't used on VATSIM.

00:00:23.262 --> 00:00:26.785
Instead, every oceanic position has an alias VHF frequency,

00:00:26.785 --> 00:00:28.756
which pilots tune in place of HF.

00:00:28.756 --> 00:00:33.222
Gander is one-three-one-point-seven, Shanwick is one-three-one-point-eight,

00:00:33.222 --> 00:00:36.081
and the NAT bandbox is one-three-one-point-nine.

00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:38.385
You'll find them in the position table.

00:00:39.685 --> 00:00:43.891
Even pilots who plan to use CPDLC must first check in with you by voice.

00:00:43.891 --> 00:00:45.877
That call includes a SELCAL check.

00:00:45.877 --> 00:00:50.376
SELCAL tells a pilot you want to speak to them, so they can turn the HF down.

00:00:50.376 --> 00:00:55.575
In the real world, you trigger four tones, and the aircraft with that code gets an alert.

00:00:55.575 --> 00:00:57.328
Here is what that sounds like.

00:00:57.328 --> 00:01:02.527
[SELCAL tones.] Two pairs of tones, standing for the four letters of the aircraft's code.

00:01:02.527 --> 00:01:05.008
On VATSIM, the pilot client does that job,

00:01:05.008 --> 00:01:07.902
using the code the pilot entered when logging in.

00:01:07.902 --> 00:01:11.349
Check SELCAL on first contact with each oceanic controller.

00:01:11.349 --> 00:01:16.548
In EuroScope, type dot selcal followed by the code, or dot selcal and click the aircraft.

00:01:16.548 --> 00:01:19.586
In vNAAATS, use the SELCAL tab in the aircraft menu.

00:01:20.886 --> 00:01:24.450
Now phraseology, using the Gander Oceanic phraseology sheet.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.430
Oceanic phraseology differs from domestic in small, important ways.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.349
Say numbers one digit at a time,

00:01:30.349 --> 00:01:34.787
and letters from the phonetic alphabet, like Speedbird one-two-four Romeo.

00:01:34.787 --> 00:01:36.866
Say Mach as Mach decimal eight-six.

00:01:36.866 --> 00:01:40.192
Say the NAT track with its phonetic letter, track alpha.

00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:43.129
Say latitudes as two digits, like five-one north,

00:01:43.129 --> 00:01:47.024
and longitudes as two or three digits, like zero-three-zero west.

00:01:47.024 --> 00:01:50.469
Never use the words super or heavy in Gander and Shanwick.

00:01:51.769 --> 00:01:54.587
When you hand an aircraft over, the word matters.

00:01:54.587 --> 00:01:56.562
For another oceanic radio station,

00:01:56.562 --> 00:02:00.338
say call, as in call Shanwick Radio on one-three-one-point-eight.

00:02:00.338 --> 00:02:05.284
For a radar controller, say contact, as in contact Moncton on one-three-two-point-two.

00:02:05.284 --> 00:02:06.895
And for UNICOM, say monitor.

00:02:08.195 --> 00:02:11.627
Here is the clearance request, from the Gander sheet.

00:02:11.627 --> 00:02:14.282
Listen to how it sounds on the frequency.

00:02:14.282 --> 00:02:19.333
Pilot: Gander Radio, Air Canada four-eight-nine, requesting oceanic clearance.

00:02:19.333 --> 00:02:23.995
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio, pass your message.

00:02:23.995 --> 00:02:30.892
Pilot: Gander Radio, Air Canada four-eight-nine, requests oceanic clearance to Boston via Nat Track alpha,

00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:37.659
estimating DOGAL at one-four-four-five, request flight level three-nine-zero and Mach decimal eight-six.

00:02:37.659 --> 00:02:42.322
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio, oceanic clearance.

00:02:42.322 --> 00:02:45.495
Pilot: Ready to copy, Air Canada four-eight-nine.

00:02:45.495 --> 00:02:48.409
A short delay to check for conflicts is fine.

00:02:48.409 --> 00:02:51.219
If the clearance isn't what they asked for,

00:02:51.219 --> 00:02:55.467
say so first, for example, oceanic clearance with a level change.

00:02:56.767 --> 00:02:59.787
Then you issue it, and the pilot reads it back.

00:02:59.787 --> 00:03:06.697
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio clears Air Canada four-eight-nine to Boston via DOGAL,

00:03:06.697 --> 00:03:12.251
Track alpha, from DOGAL maintain flight level three-nine-zero, Mach decimal eight-six.

00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:18.584
Pilot: Cleared to Boston via DOGAL, Track alpha, from DOGAL maintain flight level three-nine-zero,

00:03:18.584 --> 00:03:23.108
Mach decimal eight-six, TMI one-five-zero, Air Canada four-eight-nine.

00:03:23.108 --> 00:03:26.861
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, TMI one-five-zero,

00:03:26.861 --> 00:03:32.360
read back correct, return to previous frequency and call Gander Radio overhead DOGAL.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.544
The readback must include the TMI.

00:03:34.544 --> 00:03:36.215
If it doesn't, ask for it.

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.271
You don't give the TMI yourself.

00:03:39.571 --> 00:03:44.344
Random routes don't need a TMI, but you read out every waypoint in latitude and longitude,

00:03:44.344 --> 00:03:46.147
and the pilot reads them all back.

00:03:46.147 --> 00:03:49.231
For a Tango route, you clear the aircraft via Tango Route,

00:03:49.231 --> 00:03:51.198
routing, for example, LASNO to BEGAS.

00:03:51.198 --> 00:03:54.805
Time restrictions are added only when they're needed for separation,

00:03:54.805 --> 00:03:57.881
as in: cross MALOT not before time one-zero-zero-two Zulu.

00:03:57.881 --> 00:03:59.091
They must be read back.

00:04:00.391 --> 00:04:02.993
Now the first call on your frequency.

00:04:02.993 --> 00:04:07.213
An ADS-B equipped aircraft reports overhead its entry point.

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:10.870
Check that the level and Mach match their clearance.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.275
Pilot: Gander Radio, Air Canada four-eight-nine,

00:04:14.275 --> 00:04:19.169
overhead MALOT, flight level three-five-zero, Mach decimal eight-two.

00:04:19.169 --> 00:04:22.922
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio,

00:04:22.922 --> 00:04:29.648
position MALOT, flight level three-five-zero, Mach decimal eight-two, standby for SELCAL check.

00:04:29.648 --> 00:04:34.501
[SELCAL tones.] Pilot: SELCAL check okay, Air Canada four-eight-nine.

00:04:34.501 --> 00:04:37.272
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine,

00:04:37.272 --> 00:04:41.534
roger, remain on SELCAL watch, no position reports required.

00:04:41.534 --> 00:04:43.433
CPDLC is available at CZQX.

00:04:43.433 --> 00:04:47.695
After the SELCAL check, tell them to remain on SELCAL watch,

00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:50.607
and give them the station code for CPDLC.

00:04:50.607 --> 00:04:55.671
That's CZQX for Gander, EGGX for Shanwick, and NATX for the NAT bandbox.

00:04:56.971 --> 00:05:00.526
Aircraft that aren't ADS-B equipped check in the same way,

00:05:00.526 --> 00:05:03.162
but you ask them to pass a position report.

00:05:03.162 --> 00:05:04.819
They give the fix and time,

00:05:04.819 --> 00:05:08.868
the level, the next fix with its estimate, and the fix after that.

00:05:08.868 --> 00:05:14.149
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio, please pass your position report.

00:05:14.149 --> 00:05:18.908
Pilot: Passing DINIM at time one-nine-five-nine, flight level three-five-zero,

00:05:18.908 --> 00:05:26.229
estimating five-one north zero-two-zero west at time two-zero-two-seven, five-two north zero-three-zero west thereafter.

00:05:26.229 --> 00:05:32.445
Controller: Air Canada four-eight-nine, Gander Radio copies position DINIM at time one-nine-five-nine,

00:05:32.445 --> 00:05:41.587
flight level three-five-zero, estimating five-one north zero-two-zero west at time two-zero-two-seven, five-two north zero-three-zero west thereafter.

00:05:41.587 --> 00:05:44.036
They repeat the report at each waypoint,

00:05:44.036 --> 00:05:48.689
and you give the next sector's details after their last report in your area.

00:05:48.689 --> 00:05:51.511
On VATSIM, voice position reports are optional

00:05:51.511 --> 00:05:54.334
for ADS-B aircraft and mandatory for the rest.

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:58.826
A pilot can also call with a revised estimate, and you read that back too.

00:06:00.126 --> 00:06:05.131
For a level change, an ADS-B aircraft that asks to climb to flight level three-seven-zero

00:06:05.131 --> 00:06:09.798
hears: Gander Radio clears you to climb and maintain flight level three-seven-zero.

00:06:09.798 --> 00:06:12.090
For other aircraft, add: report reaching.

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:15.816
And at the boundary, use the right word: call for a radio station,

00:06:15.816 --> 00:06:17.961
contact for radar, monitor for UNICOM.

00:06:19.261 --> 00:06:19.984
Next, CPDLC.

00:06:19.984 --> 00:06:22.940
It's text messaging between pilot and controller.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:24.708
On VATSIM it isn't mandatory,

00:06:24.708 --> 00:06:28.428
but ADS-B equipped aircraft are assumed to be able to use it.

00:06:28.428 --> 00:06:31.565
On the network it runs over the Hoppie ACARS system.

00:06:31.565 --> 00:06:35.666
You need your own Hoppie logon code, which you get at hoppie dot nl.

00:06:36.966 --> 00:06:43.845
In vNAAATS, open the CPDLC drawer, enter your code, and the station is chosen from your position: CZQX for Gander,

00:06:43.845 --> 00:06:46.499
EGGX for Shanwick, and NATX for the bandbox.

00:06:46.499 --> 00:06:48.958
Only one controller can use each station.

00:06:48.958 --> 00:06:51.195
If you use the Topsky plugin instead,

00:06:51.195 --> 00:06:56.273
turn Auto-accept logon off, so no aircraft logs on before you've made voice contact.

00:06:57.573 --> 00:07:02.240
The rule is simple: voice contact and a SELCAL check first, then accept the logon.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:05.143
A new logon plays a sound and flashes the callsign.

00:07:05.143 --> 00:07:08.957
A logged-on aircraft has solid square brackets around its callsign.

00:07:08.957 --> 00:07:13.226
From then on, the level and Mach you change are sent to the pilot by CPDLC.

00:07:13.226 --> 00:07:16.641
Brackets show on the new data until the pilot answers WILCO.

00:07:16.641 --> 00:07:18.861
If they say UNABLE, the change reverts.

00:07:18.861 --> 00:07:21.793
If you've already agreed a change on the frequency,

00:07:21.793 --> 00:07:24.724
choose the voice option so you don't send it twice.

00:07:26.024 --> 00:07:29.821
You can reply with quick buttons, such as unable, standby and roger.

00:07:29.821 --> 00:07:34.455
To hand off, choose the next controller, and the pilot is sent the contact message.

00:07:34.455 --> 00:07:36.707
When no further controller is available,

00:07:36.707 --> 00:07:41.491
use the UNICOM message, and let the pilot answer before you end the CPDLC connection.

00:07:42.791 --> 00:07:46.215
Pilots can also request their oceanic clearance by CPDLC.

00:07:46.215 --> 00:07:49.731
Only controllers using vNAAATS can receive these requests,

00:07:49.731 --> 00:07:52.884
and you can use them instead of the natTRAK website.

00:07:52.884 --> 00:07:57.600
The request arrives by CPDLC, and you then write it into natTRAK from vNAAATS,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.436
so the conflict tool still sees the aircraft, and issue the clearance as normal.

00:08:02.436 --> 00:08:06.736
If you are taking requests this way, put it in your controller details,

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:10.006
so pilots know: Oceanic Clearance via CPDLC available.

00:08:11.306 --> 00:08:13.874
Now vNAAATS, the toolset you'll use to control.

00:08:13.874 --> 00:08:18.171
It has three parts that share one set of flight data: the Flight Data Display,

00:08:18.171 --> 00:08:20.540
a EuroScope plugin, and the vNAAATS server.

00:08:20.540 --> 00:08:23.522
Because of that, every controller sees the same level,

00:08:23.522 --> 00:08:25.621
Mach, track and SELCAL within seconds.

00:08:26.921 --> 00:08:29.753
The Flight Data Display, the FDD, is where you work.

00:08:29.753 --> 00:08:33.184
Every oceanic flight is a strip, grouped by track or entry fix.

00:08:33.184 --> 00:08:38.086
You can change flight data, track and transfer aircraft, send SELCAL checks and run CPDLC.

00:08:38.086 --> 00:08:40.210
Click a callsign for the aircraft menu.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.661
The display doesn't check separation for you.

00:08:42.661 --> 00:08:43.859
That's still your job.

00:08:45.159 --> 00:08:48.895
To get started, get your personal key at vnaaats dot com slash get-key.

00:08:48.895 --> 00:08:52.946
Gander controllers can also take it from their profile on the Gander website.

00:08:52.946 --> 00:08:54.050
It lasts ninety days.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:56.705
Install FDD for Windows, which sets up the plugin,

00:08:56.705 --> 00:08:59.837
the airline list and the font, and opens EuroScope for you.

00:08:59.837 --> 00:09:02.994
Follow the setup screen, and it takes about fifteen minutes.

00:09:04.294 --> 00:09:05.771
Keeping up to date is easy.

00:09:05.771 --> 00:09:08.423
The latest updates are posted as messages in the

00:09:08.423 --> 00:09:10.966
vNAAATS section of the Gander Oceanic Discord.

00:09:10.966 --> 00:09:14.575
And with FDD for Windows, it will prompt you if there's an update.

00:09:14.575 --> 00:09:17.692
Confirm it, and it installs automatically within seconds.

00:09:17.692 --> 00:09:21.555
Once you have set up your EuroScope profile to use the vNAAATS plugin,

00:09:21.555 --> 00:09:23.707
make sure you save the profile on exit.

00:09:25.007 --> 00:09:28.390
We know that some controllers come from a domestic background,

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.008
where EuroScope is the native controller client.

00:09:31.008 --> 00:09:32.900
vNAAATS is built with this in mind.

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:37.171
EuroScope is only used as your connection to VATSIM, and as a visual reference.

00:09:37.171 --> 00:09:39.549
All the work is done from the strips in FDD.

00:09:39.549 --> 00:09:42.335
Your instructor will also show you some of the more

00:09:42.335 --> 00:09:44.847
technical features of FDD during your session.

00:09:46.147 --> 00:09:50.682
Some aircraft report by voice, like Concorde or a flight that isn't ADS-B equipped.

00:09:50.682 --> 00:09:53.929
In FDD, open the aircraft menu and switch on Voice reports,

00:09:53.929 --> 00:09:58.003
then type each report in the Position reports table as the pilot gives it.

00:09:58.003 --> 00:10:01.718
On the EuroScope screen, a voice report doesn't freeze the aircraft.

00:10:01.718 --> 00:10:03.575
It corrects the system's estimate.

00:10:03.575 --> 00:10:08.274
If nobody is tracking the aircraft, its tag is green and moves with its real position.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:11.442
Once it is tracked, the tag follows a calculated position.

00:10:11.442 --> 00:10:16.347
The last report anchors it, and the tag then moves along the direct line to the next fix,

00:10:16.347 --> 00:10:17.725
arriving at the estimate.

00:10:17.725 --> 00:10:19.200
A new report re-anchors it.

00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:23.134
The system can't see weather deviations, so it keeps to the direct path.

00:10:23.134 --> 00:10:26.717
If the estimate passes by more than three minutes with no report,

00:10:26.717 --> 00:10:29.143
the tag holds at that fix and flashes white.

00:10:29.143 --> 00:10:31.820
No conflict alerts are raised for these aircraft.

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:35.625
And if you track an aircraft that is already in the oceanic airspace,

00:10:35.625 --> 00:10:37.775
ignore the fixes it has already passed.

00:10:37.775 --> 00:10:39.469
No reports are needed for them.

00:10:40.769 --> 00:10:44.137
Please set up your EuroScope profile before your live session.

00:10:44.137 --> 00:10:48.158
You won't be able to test the connection until you're with the instructor.

00:10:48.158 --> 00:10:54.016
If your FDD shows awaiting a connection, and EuroScope shows the blue bar and a visual map of the NAT area,

00:10:54.016 --> 00:10:55.275
then you're good to go.

00:10:56.575 --> 00:11:00.909
The natTRAK boxes show pending and cleared requests, with a sound for new requests.

00:11:00.909 --> 00:11:05.642
The display can also send the pilot an automatic reminder to request clearance on natTRAK,

00:11:05.642 --> 00:11:08.113
at least thirty minutes before the entry point.

00:11:08.113 --> 00:11:11.847
If something goes wrong, check the problems page in the Knowledge Base,

00:11:11.847 --> 00:11:15.370
and the full manual and videos are at vnaaats dot com slash manual.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:17.637
That's everything.

00:11:17.637 --> 00:11:20.292
When you pass the exam, submit your availability,

00:11:20.292 --> 00:11:23.598
and an instructor will book your live session on the network.

00:11:23.598 --> 00:11:28.194
Under a shared agreement with VATSIM UK, you're trained for both Gander and Shanwick,

00:11:28.194 --> 00:11:31.600
so your live session is taken on NAT_FSS and covers both sides.

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.772
Bring what you've learned: the airspace,

00:11:33.772 --> 00:11:36.541
the routes, separation, phraseology and your tools.

00:11:36.541 --> 00:11:39.197
The instructor will guide you, and when you pass,

00:11:39.197 --> 00:11:42.449
you're given your Oceanic endorsement and your Discord role.

00:11:42.449 --> 00:11:43.523
Welcome to the team.
